   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( P_3(x) \\) be the Taylor polynomial of degree 3 for a function f about \\( x = 1 \\). On the interval \\( 1 \\le x \\le \\frac{5}{3} \\), \\( f^{(4)}(x) \\) is positive and increasing, with \\( f^{(4)}(1) = 4 \\) and \\( f^{(4)}(\\frac{5}{3}) = 12 \\). Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for \\( \\left|f(\\frac{5}{3}) - P_3(\\frac{5}{3})\\right| \\).",
      "command_verb": "find"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         8,
         81
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "A polynomial of degree 3 needs the bound M on the fourth derivative. An increasing positive function is largest at the right end of the interval, so \\( M = f^{(4)}(\\frac{5}{3}) = 12 \\).",
         "rule_named": "order of the bounding derivative"
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The Lagrange error bound is \\( \\left|f(\\frac{5}{3}) - P_3(\\frac{5}{3})\\right| \\le \\frac{M}{4!} \\left|\\frac{5}{3} - 1\\right|^{4} \\).",
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99039",
         "rule_named": "Lagrange error bound form"
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "With \\( M = 12 \\) and distance \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\), the bound is \\( \\frac{12}{24} \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{4} = \\frac{8}{81} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            8,
            81
         ],
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99041",
         "rule_named": "evaluate the bound"
      }
